WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.580
Library assurance needs more than happy-path tests.

2
00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:17.720
Unit tests and integration tests should cover normal use, likely misuse, invalid parameters, boundary sizes, malformed input, resource limits, compatibility behavior, and cleanup after failure.

3
00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:24.620
If the library supports multiple platforms or build modes, tests should include the combinations consumers actually use.

4
00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:28.960
Test names should make the intended contract easy to understand.

5
00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:34.360
Defensive fuzzing is useful for parsers and other input boundaries.

6
00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:42.140
The goal is to discover unexpected crashes, hangs, excessive resource use, and invariant violations before consumers encounter them.

7
00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:52.400
Sanitizers, static analysis, and CI checks can add more signal when they are tied to ownership and triage rules instead of producing unreviewed noise.

8
00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:55.700
Findings should feed the same issue process as other quality work.

9
00:00:55.980 --> 00:00:59.180
Documentation is a security control for libraries.

10
00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:09.280
Callers need accurate guidance on ownership, lifetime, error behavior, thread safety, supported platforms, configuration, compatibility, and advanced APIs.

11
00:01:09.380 --> 00:01:21.060
If documentation is missing, callers will infer behavior from examples, tests, or private implementation details, and those inferences may not match the library's intended contract.

12
00:01:21.160 --> 00:01:24.440
Documentation should also name unsupported uses.

13
00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:29.420
Secure examples should show recommended configuration, cleanup, and error handling.

14
00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:32.260
Release governance keeps trust over time.

15
00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:44.360
Changelogs, release notes, deprecation notices, vulnerability reporting channels, advisory processes, patch branches, and rollback guidance all help consumers update safely.

16
00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:47.420
A secure library is maintained, not merely written.

17
00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:53.600
Consumers need to know what changed, why it changed, what risks were addressed, and how to move forward.

18
00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:57.460
Governance gives them confidence to adopt safer versions promptly.

19
00:01:57.560 --> 00:02:03.880
It also gives maintainers a repeatable way to handle urgent fixes and communicate them clearly to downstream teams.

20
00:02:03.980 --> 00:02:12.940
That governance should identify decision owners, severity expectations, disclosure paths, and supported update windows before an urgent issue arrives.

21
00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:18.720
It should also define how consumers receive migration guidance for patched releases safely.
